  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1045 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nTonight and Wednesday:\n\nWater vapor imagery this evening continues to show the compact \nmid/upper low continuing its push into the Great Lakes region while \nsandwiching a ridge between it and a Pacific Coastal upper low.\nA cold front is being pulled through the area, scouring out \ndewpoints with the some values in the 50s trying to sneak into \nnortheast Nebraska. Along/ahead of it, winds have become calm \nand efficient cooling has already begun to push dewpoints past \ncrossover temperatures with fog being the main concern of the \nshort-term forecast. Those lower dewpoints will continue to move\nin from the northwest, but the drier advance will take some \ntime to get to the IA/NE/MO border region where fog is expected \nto be most dense. Already, lower visibilities have been reported\nacross eastern Nebraska and Iowa (KOFK, KFET, KRDK, and KICL) \nof 5 miles or less, with further deterioration expected through \nthe night. A Dense Fog Advisory will likely be issued for that \narea with 5-6 AM expected to hold the thickest fog. \n\nAs the sun begins stirring the boundary layer and dry air filters in \nfrom the northwest, foggy morning conditions will dissipate and\nshift southeast. Once it does so, mostly clear skies, a ridge \nof high surface pressure, and continued north-northwesterly \nwinds settle in for the remainder of the day, with highs in low-\nto-mid 80s on tap for the afternoon. Shower and storm activity \nwon't be too far off, largely being contained along and west of \nthe instability gradient that arcs up from north-central Kansas \nthrough western Nebraska. Heading into the overnight hours, \nwe'll be on the lookout for elevated showers and storms that \nwould develop along the convergence on the nose of the low-level\njet, but those chances largely look to stay west and south of \nthe forecast area. \n\nThursday and Beyond:\n\nThursday heading into the weekend will see a bit of a pattern \nchange, as the long-standing mid/upper ridge to the west erodes and \neventually is swept eastward by a trough incoming from the Pacific. \nOur coolest highs of the forecast are set for Thursday, followed by \na warming trend that takes highs into the mid-to-upper 80s Friday, \nand into the 90s Saturday, Sunday, and Monday. Some of the rain-\nmakers for this period arrive late Friday/early Saturday in a \nleading shortwave, followed by a frontal boundary that stalls out \nover the general area late Sunday into Monday. Next week could trend \ntowards increased rainfall, with several of the deterministic models \nholding southwesterly flow over the Central Plains in anticipation \nof a deeper trough moving through later in the week. There's still \nplenty of time for details to come into better focus, but for now, \nsevere storm lovers may have to wait until next week before chances \nreturn to the forecast area.",
